    "tranche": "T3",
    "tranche_reason": "Tests an analogous scaling result in a physical effective-model setting rather than language-model pretraining: estimated MSE is plotted against trainable parameter count for an equivariant Transformer used in self-learning Monte Carlo.",
    "gate_note": "The paper cites language-model scaling laws as background, but its own scaling results concern an effective model for the double-exchange physical system. The physical-observable and autocorrelation results do not test a listed claim version. The paper reports estimated MSE points for the linear model and L=1–6 in Fig. 6, and acceptance-ratio points for the same layer counts in Fig. 4.",
    "test_reason": "Fits a power law of estimated MSE against trainable-parameter count for an equivariant Transformer effective model in self-learning Monte Carlo. That is a new, non-text setting with its own result on whether the power-law form carries over, so it bears on V5. It does not bear on language-model pretraining (V1–V4, V6, V7).",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly right. The authors say their equivariant Transformer overcomes the linear model's poor acceptance and shows a scaling law similar to large language models, with MSE decreasing as trainable parameters (layers) increase. Their Summary instead says MSE falls with 'number of training data'. The actual Fig. 6 result is against parameter count; no data-size scaling is measured.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "Measures estimated MSE for a physical effective model as attention-layer count and trainable parameter count vary, rather than held-out language-model cross-entropy across parameters, tokens, or compute. The paper fits only L≥2 points as a guide to the eye.",
    "method_sensitivity": "",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "SLMC acceptance ratio rises monotonically with attention-layer count, from about 21% for the linear model (Fig. 4, NMC_eff=100).",
        "proposed_claim": "Sampler acceptance rate of ML effective models in Monte Carlo improves monotonically with model depth/capacity.",
        "row_ids": [
          "R8",
          "R9",
          "R10",
          "R11",
          "R12",
          "R13",
          "R14"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "controls": "n",
    "capability_check": "y",
    "uncertainty_reported": "n",
    "code_released": "not stated",
    "peer_reviewed": "Journal of the Physical Society of Japan",
    "problems": "The scaling evidence is confined to one physical system and a small layer-count sweep. Fig. 6 gives no numerical MSE values or fitted exponents in the text, and the fit is only a guide to the eye using L≥2 points; no uncertainty is reported.",
    "rows_left_out": "No relevant scaling results left out. Physical observables and autocorrelation results in Sections V.B–V.C and V.E are not results on a listed claim version.",
    "untested": [
      {
